{"text": " So if I were to create a frozen yogurt store, this is how I would do it. So when I was 21, I was deciding between this, test prep, and fitness. So number one, I would open my own frozen yogurt store, and so here are the numbers on the top five frozen yogurt franchises in the US. Owner pay, as you can see, after everything, super slim. Number two, store setup. So most frozen yogurt store owners charge 25 cents to 60 cents per ounce, and yogurt costs about eight cents per ounce, and that's pretty high margin. Toppings cost, on the other hand, 10 to 40 cents per ounce, which is typically low margin. Since the average cup size is eight ounces, I would line the store up from highest to lowest margin. And so you grab your cup, you choose your yogurt, then you choose your hard toppings, then you choose your fresh toppings. So number three, cup size. I would only serve medium and large cups, because when you give someone a bigger cup, they're more likely to fill it. If you have a bucket like this, and then you have to eat frozen yogurt out of it, and the bucket's this big, you're like, well, shoot, I gotta kind of fill it up a little bit, otherwise it looks dumb. Number four, self-serve. So most business owners charge for small, medium, large, but what actually is super smart in this industry specifically is they charge by weight, which the consumer is in control of. Number five, I would try and become the Chick-fil-A of the industry. So Yogurtland, Menchies, Goldenspoon, they're all almost exactly the same. There is no Chick-fil-A in the business, which you can do with these three things. And so number six, marketing. So I would set up close to colleges and partner with fraternities and sororities and have competitions between them to see who could get the most yogurt and give them some sort of merch or the most yogurt during that period of time. And I'd also have crazy, like, 50% off types incentives to get customer numbers in, so I could acquire a lead that I 